{"name":"Floating Island","hex":"#ece5cf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"floating island","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An aggregation of driftwood and roots, or vegetation, capable of bearing soil, which floats on the surface of (bog or lake) water or is floated out from a river delta or similar.","A French cold dessert consisting of an \"island\" of meringue, made of egg whites and sugar, floating in a \"sea\" of custard."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/floating_island"}],"entries":[{"title":"Floating island","blocks":[{"text":"A floating island is a mass of floating aquatic plants, mud, and peat ranging in thickness from several centimeters to a few meters. Sometimes referred to as tussocks, floatons, or suds, floating islands are found in many parts of the world. They exist less commonly as an artificial island. Floating islands are generally found on marshlands, lakes, and similar wetland locations, and can be many hectares in size."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b8/Natural_floating_island.jpg/330px-Natural_floating_island.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating_island"},{"title":"Floating island (dessert)","blocks":[{"text":"A floating island or île flottante is a dessert consisting of soft meringue floating on crème anglaise. The meringue used is baked in a bain-marie. It may be served at room temperature or chilled."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ujuvad_saarekesed.jpg/330px-Ujuvad_saarekesed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating_island_(dessert)"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Uros-floating-islands-puno-peru-aerial.jpg/500px-Uros-floating-islands-puno-peru-aerial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Uros-floating-islands-puno-peru-aerial.jpg/1280px-Uros-floating-islands-puno-peru-aerial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Uros-floating-islands-puno-peru-aerial.jpg/500px-Uros-floating-islands-puno-peru-aerial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Artificial floating island communities of the Uru peoples in Lake Titicaca","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUros-floating-islands-puno-peru-aerial.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Isola_galleggiante_Posta_Fibreno_picc.jpg/500px-Isola_galleggiante_Posta_Fibreno_picc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Isola_galleggiante_Posta_Fibreno_picc.jpg/1280px-Isola_galleggiante_Posta_Fibreno_picc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbna